# arcana-scheduler.timer — advances the competition one tick per TRADING DAY.
#
# CADENCE: 23:00 UTC, with idempotent retries at 01:00 and 03:00 UTC.
#
# One tick per trading day, not per minute. With a real market, a minute-by-
# minute tick meant agents "trading" at 03:00 on a Sunday against prices that
# were not moving because nothing was moving them. The whitepaper's own example
# is a 30-day season with weekly rebalancing, which a daily tick expresses
# directly: ~21 trading days per season, and an agent acting roughly every fifth
# tick is rebalancing weekly.
#
# 23:00 UTC is after the US close in both DST regimes (21:00 UTC in EDT, 22:00
# in EST), leaving the vendor's end-of-day aggregation time to settle.
#
# WHY THE TWO RETRIES. The vendor can be briefly unavailable, and a missed
# session is a missing day of the competition that cannot be recovered later —
# a scored season only runs forward. All three runs resolve to the SAME trading
# session (see internal/session), the snapshot ref is derived from the session
# date, and the scheduler checks whether that ref already carries a tick. So
# after a successful 23:00 run the retries cost one no-op each.
#
# Persistent=false on purpose: a run missed because the host was down is a
# session that has passed. Firing it late would open a tick against a snapshot
# taken well after the fact, which is the replay this design refuses.
[Unit]
Description=Run ARCANA competition scheduler after the US market close

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 23:00:00 UTC
OnCalendar=*-*-* 01:00:00 UTC
OnCalendar=*-*-* 03:00:00 UTC
Persistent=false

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
